    "description": "Enterprise AI concerns the organizational use of artificial intelligence, including responsibility for evaluation, deployment, and ongoing risk management. Iranian forecasting research supplies examples of relevant technical work, but the inspected evidence does not establish nationwide enterprise adoption or measured commercial returns.",
    "tags": ["Enterprise AI", "AI governance", "Organizational adoption", "Model evaluation", "Iranian businesses"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T19:29:24Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T19:29:24Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Enterprise AI in Iran: Deployment, Governance, and Evidence Gaps",
      "description": "An evidence-limited introduction to enterprise AI in Iran, distinguishing technical research, organizational deployment, and verified business outcomes."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "organizational-scope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From a model to an organizational responsibility",
        "content": "Enterprise AI places artificial intelligence within an organization's activities and responsibilities. A useful reference is the AI Risk Management Framework published by the United States National Institute of Standards and Technology, or NIST. It addresses organizations designing, developing, deploying, or using AI and is intended to work across sectors and organization sizes. It is a voluntary framework, not a statement of Iranian law or evidence that Iranian businesses follow it. [s1]\n\nThis distinction helps international readers assess the Iranian business landscape without equating technical capability with organizational adoption. Research can establish that a method was tested; deployment requires separate evidence about who used it, in which workflow, and under what controls. This page uses the framework as an explanatory reference and keeps Iran-specific claims within the limits of the inspected research. [s1][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iran-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the Iranian evidence establishes",
        "content": "Electricity forecasting illustrates a task relevant to organizational decision-making. A study recorded by the University of Helsinki used Iranian Grid Management Company load data and compared forecasting methods across different horizons and conditions. This supports a specific claim about research using Iranian operational data. The inspected abstract does not establish that the company deployed the resulting model, purchased a service, or achieved a measured financial return. [s4]\n\nThat boundary matters for a business-opportunities guide. A published experiment can identify a technically relevant problem without establishing a supplier's customer base or a market's size. Company case studies, procurement records, and independently assessed outcomes would be needed to develop a fuller account of enterprise use in Iran. Those remain research requirements rather than findings of this page. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "governance-reference",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A framework for understanding deployment",
        "content": "NIST organizes risk management around Govern, Map, Measure, and Manage. Governance crosses the other functions: responsibilities and policies shape how an organization understands an application, evaluates it, and responds to risks. The framework explicitly says these functions are not necessarily an ordered checklist. Monitoring and improvement continue after deployment. [s2]\n\nFor an enterprise reader, the implication is that a model's evaluation score is only part of the account. The organization also needs to understand the application context and decide how identified risks will be treated. This is an editorial explanation of the framework, not a claim about practices already established in Iranian firms. [s2]\n\nThe companion NIST Playbook offers suggested actions and references that organizations can tailor to their circumstances. Its flexibility is useful when explaining why enterprise AI cannot be reduced to buying a model or adopting a single organizational template. It provides a vocabulary for examining deployment; it does not certify a product or supply missing evidence about Iranian adoption. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "governance-diagram",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "NIST risk-management functions as an explanatory reference",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  G[\"Govern: responsibilities and policies\"] -->|\"Informs\"| A[\"Map: application context and risks\"]\n  G -->|\"Informs\"| B[\"Measure: assess identified risks\"]\n  G -->|\"Informs\"| C[\"Manage: prioritize and treat risks\"]\n  C -->|\"Continues after deployment\"| D[\"Monitoring and improvement\"]\n```",
        "summary": "This simplified diagram shows governance informing the other NIST functions and management continuing after deployment. The arrows describe an explanatory framework, not a mandatory sequence, Iranian legal authority, or verified Iranian company practice.",
        "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      }
    ],
